There is an integer $\ell\ge1$, depending only on $b,J$, with the following properties. Given any finite order $p_*>0$ and a sufficiently small prescribed number $\delta_{\rm tree}>0$, one can choose $h,w_0>0$ so that, uniformly for $e\in J$, $0<w\le w_0$, and $0<\eta\le h$, $$\label{eq:tree-small-moments}
 \|Y(e,\eta)\|_2\le\delta_{\rm tree},\qquad
 \mathbb ED(Y(e,\eta))^p\le K_p\quad(0<p\le p_*).$$ The choices also include the additional fixed finite moment orders required by the stability assertion that follows. There are constants $\delta_*>0$ and $C<\infty$ such that, if a probability law $P$ on $\mathbb D$ satisfies $\int|x|^2\,dP(x)\le\delta_*^2$ and $$W_2\bigl(P,\mathop{\mathrm{Law}}(F_j(X_1,\ldots,X_{b^j}))\bigr)\le u,
 \qquad j=1,\ell,$$ where the $X_i$ are independent with law $P$, then $$\label{eq:tree-law-stability}
 W_2(P,\mathop{\mathrm{Law}}(Y(e,\eta)))\le Cu.$$ The constants $K_p,\delta_*,C$ can be fixed before decreasing the parameters further to make $\delta_{\rm tree}$ much smaller than $\delta_*$. On a fixed tree with its fixed potential variables, $(e,\eta)\mapsto Y(e,\eta)$ extends continuously in $L^2$ to $J\times[0,h]$. The boundary values belong to $\mathbb D$ almost surely, obey <a href="#eq:tree-small-moments" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:tree-small-moments">[eq:tree-small-moments]</a>, and are measurable functions of the potentials.

There are $c,C>0$ and $y_0<\infty$ such that, for $y=n\eta\ge y_0$, $$\label{eq:distributional-local-law}
 \mathbb P\left\{
 W_2(P_H,Q_\zeta)+W_2(P_H^0,Q_\zeta^0)>y^{-c}
 \right\}\le Cy^{-c}.$$ Here $W_2$ is transportation distance for the Euclidean metric on the disk. The estimate is uniform in $e\in J$ and $0<\eta\le h$.

Let $H=A+\mathop{\mathrm{diag}}(V_v)$, where $A$ is a real symmetric matrix independent of the iid uniform potentials $V_v\in[-w,w]$. For $\Im\zeta>0$, $$\label{eq:site-averaging}
 \mathbb E\,\Im G_{vv}(\zeta)\le C_w,
 \qquad
 \mathbb E\,\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}\mathbf1_I(H)\le C_w n|I|$$ for every interval $I\subset\mathbb R$. If $S$ consists of at most $k$ vertices chosen without reference to the potentials, then $$\label{eq:compression-tail}
 \mathbb P\{\|G_{SS}(\zeta)\|>D\}\le C_{w,k}D^{-1/2},\qquad D\ge1.$$ The same conclusions hold after any graph modifications chosen independently of the potentials. The compression estimate also holds for the inverse of a fixed strictly dissipative $k\times k$ matrix plus an iid uniform real diagonal; its constant is independent of that matrix.

Fix $E$ in the interior of $J$ and $s\in\mathbb R$. The following statements hold.*

1.  *At $\zeta=E+(s+iy)/n$, in the order $n\to\infty$ and then $y\to\infty$, the diagonal $G_{vv}(\zeta)$ at a uniform vertex, jointly with every fixed ordered neighborhood of potentials around $v$, converges to the tree diagonal $m_o(E+i0)$ with the corresponding tree environment. For any fixed number of independently sampled vertices the joint assertion holds with independent tree environments, and their off-diagonal Green entries tend to zero in probability. The diagonal assertion also holds along every sequence $n\eta\to\infty$, $(e,\eta)\to(E,0)$.*


2.  *There are $c,C>0$ such that, for every sufficiently large fixed $y$, $$\label{eq:trace-slow-power}
     \limsup_{n\to\infty}\mathbb P\left\{
     \left|\Im\frac1n\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}G\left(E+\frac{s+iy}{n}\right)
                 -\pi\rho_{d,w}(E)\right|>y^{-c}\right\}\le Cy^{-c}.$$ The constants are independent of the fixed real number $s$.*


3.  *For each fixed $s\in\mathbb R$ and $y>0$, the sampled diagonal imaginary parts are uniformly integrable in the following limiting sense: $$\label{eq:microscopic-ui}
     \lim_{T\to\infty}\limsup_{n\to\infty}
     \mathbb E_{H,v}\left[
     \Im G_{vv}\left(E+\frac{s+iy}{n}\right)
     \mathbf1_{\{\Im G_{vv}(E+(s+iy)/n)>T\}}\right]=0.$$*

*The double-limit assertion in the first item means that, for bounded continuous tests, the limsup of the discrepancy as $n\to\infty$ tends to zero as $y\to\infty$.

For almost every empirical law in Proposition <a href="#prop:marked-limits" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:marked-limits">10</a>, $$\label{eq:conditional-zero-mean}
 \mathbb E_{\mathcal L}[U_v\mid\omega]=0
 \qquad\text{at every vertex }v.$$

The centered Gaussian with covariance $K_o$ is supported on the $d$-dimensional hyperplane $$\label{eq:star-constraint}
 C:=(V_o-E)Z+\sum_{i=1}^dX_i=0.$$ In the coordinate volume $\delta(C)\,dZ\prod_i dX_i$, equivalently the volume obtained by forgetting any one neighbor, its precision quadratic form is $$\label{eq:star-precision}
 \sum_{i=1}^d\frac{|X_i+g_iZ|^2}{b_i}.$$ Write $K_o^{\rm ret}$ for its covariance in coordinates forgetting neighbor $d$. Then $$\label{eq:star-determinant}
 \det K_o^{\rm ret}=|m_o|^2\prod_{i=1}^d b_i,
 \qquad
 h_o^{\rm G}:=\mathcal H(N(0,K_o^{\rm ret}))
 =\frac d2\log(2\pi\mathrm e)+\frac12\sum_i\log b_i+\log|m_o|.$$ If $g'$ is the cavity message from the $o$-side of the edge $(o,i)$, its edge precision and determinant are $$\begin{aligned}
 (Z,X_i)K_{oi}^{-1}(Z,X_i)^{\mathsf T}
 &=\frac{|X_i+g_iZ|^2}{b_i}
       +\frac{|Z+g'X_i|^2}{\Im g'},\label{eq:edge-precision}\\
 \det K_{oi}&=\frac{b_i\Im g'}{|1-g_i g'|^2}.
 \label{eq:edge-determinant}
\end{aligned}$$ In particular each edge covariance is positive definite.

At positive height, with all tree Green functions evaluated at $\zeta$, $$\label{eq:tree-log-potential}
 -U(\zeta)=\mathbb E_\omega\log|m_o|
       +\frac d2\mathbb E_\omega\log|1-g_i g'|.$$ Both sides have finite limits as $\zeta=E+i\eta$, $\eta\downarrow0$. Denote the limit of $U$ by $U(E)$. With $h_{oi}^{\rm G}=\mathcal H(N(0,K_{oi}))$, $$\label{eq:gaussian-entropy-constant}
 \mathbb E_\omega\left[h_o^{\rm G}-\frac12\sum_{i=1}^d h_{oi}^{\rm G}\right]
 =-U(E).$$

Within almost every empirical law $\mathcal L$ of Proposition <a href="#prop:marked-limits" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:marked-limits">10</a>, the conditional root marks have law $$\label{eq:conditional-gaussian-marks}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Law}}_{\mathcal L}(U_o\mid\omega)
 =N\left(0,\frac{\Im m_o(E+i0)}{\pi\rho}\mathrm I_m\right).$$ In particular the colors are conditionally independent. For any finite collection of independently sampled roots and any retained finite position list, their environments are iid and independent of the limiting point process $\nu$. Conditional on these environments and $\nu$, all their marks are independent centered Gaussians with the variances in <a href="#eq:conditional-gaussian-marks" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:conditional-gaussian-marks">[eq:conditional-gaussian-marks]</a>. Positions of multiplicity greater than one have one separate color per occurrence.

There is a deterministic $\chi>0$ such that, almost surely, $$\label{eq:discrepancy}
 \nu([v,u])=\rho(u-v)+O(R^{1-\chi}),
 \qquad -R\le v\le u\le R,\qquad R\longrightarrow\infty,$$ uniformly in $v,u$. The implicit constant may depend on the realization. The same conclusion holds with any endpoint convention. In particular, $$\label{eq:position-tail-bounds}
 \mathop{\mathrm{PV}}\sum_{|x_j|>R}\frac1{x_j}=O(R^{-\chi}),\qquad
 \sum_{|x_j|>R}\frac1{x_j^2}=O(R^{-1}).$$ Here and below $\mathop{\mathrm{PV}}$ means symmetric truncation in the position: $\lim_{L\to\infty}\sum_{|x_j|\le L}$.

Fix $k$. At site $\alpha\le k$, fix a measure-preserving map $T_\alpha:[-w,w]\to[-w,w]$ and an event $B_\alpha$ measurable in its root-excluded tree environment. Set $$D_\alpha=\boldsymbol1_{B_\alpha}
                 \bigl(T_\alpha(V_\alpha)-V_\alpha\bigr),
             \qquad D=\mathop{\mathrm{diag}}(D_1,\ldots,D_k).$$ Then, for every finite set of arguments in $\mathbb H$, the imaginary parts of $$\label{eq:potential-switch-transform}
       m(z)-\partial_z\log\det(\mathrm I+D M_k(z))$$ have jointly the same law as the corresponding imaginary parts of $m(z)$. The identity is unconditional. In particular it applies to piecewise translations and to arbitrary measurable root-excluded selections of whether to perform them.

On the preceding extension of any microscopic subsequential law, let $\mathcal W_r$ be the upper-left $m_I$-dimensional block of an infinite raw GOE array independent of $\mathcal E$, and set $$\label{eq:insertion-matrix}
 H_r=D_I+s_1\mathrm I_{m_I}+\sqrt{s_2}\,\mathcal W_r.$$ Then, along $r=2^j$, the raw eigenvalue counting measures $$\nu^{(r)}=\sum_{j=1}^{m_I}\delta_{\lambda_j(H_r)}
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad\nu$$ converge in law for the local vague topology, without conditioning. Moreover $m_I=2\rho K+O(K^{1-\chi})$ almost surely, and the coefficient bounds <a href="#eq:insertion-coefficients" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:insertion-coefficients">[eq:insertion-coefficients]</a> hold eventually almost surely.

For $\mathcal A=I,F$, put $a_I=h_1$, $a_F=K$. With probability tending to one, $$\label{eq:sw-bernstein}
 \left\|U_{\mathcal A}
       (A'_{\mathcal A\mathcal A}-s_1\mathrm I)
       U_{\mathcal A}\right\|
 \le \operatorname{polylog}(r)
       \left(\sqrt{T/a_{\mathcal A}}+\theta\right).$$ Moreover, for $P_0=U_IA'_{IF}U_F$, $$\label{eq:sw-cross-block}
 \|P_0\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2\le T\operatorname{polylog}(r)$$ with probability tending to one.

Let $$\delta_F=\operatorname{polylog}(r)
       \left(\frac{1+T}{K}+\sqrt{\frac TK}+\theta\right).$$ Then $$\label{eq:sw-far-resolvent}
 \|U_F^{-1}(G_F-D_F^{-1})U_F^{-1}\|\le\delta_F=o(1)$$ with probability tending to one, and $\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}G_F=o_{\mathbb P}(1)$. For the Schur correction $\Sigma$, $$\label{eq:sw-schur-small}
 \|U_I\Sigma U_I\|=o_{\mathbb P}(T^{-5}).$$

For every fixed $z\in\mathbb H$, $$\label{eq:sw-inner-resolvent}
 \|U_I^{-1}G_I(z)U_I^{-1}\|=O_{\mathbb P}(h_1),$$ and $$\label{eq:sw-second-cut}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}(D_x+A'-z)^{-1}
 =\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}(D_I+A'_{II}-z)^{-1}+o_{\mathbb P}(1).$$ The same estimates hold jointly for a fixed finite list of $z$’s.

Conditional on almost every realization of $\mathcal E$, bounded smooth tests with bounded derivatives of finitely many trace values have expectation difference $o(1)$ between $$D_I+A'_{II}
 \quad\hbox{and}\quad
 H_r=D_I+s_1\mathrm I_{m_I}+\sqrt{s_2}\,\mathcal W.$$

Suppose a sequence of deterministic diagonal matrices $V$ of dimension $m\to\infty$ satisfies <a href="#eq:dbm-regularity" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:dbm-regularity">[eq:dbm-regularity]</a> and <a href="#eq:dbm-parameter-domain" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:dbm-parameter-domain">[eq:dbm-parameter-domain]</a>, with fixed constants. Fix $\sigma>0$, and suppose $$\label{eq:dbm-time-window}
 g m^\sigma\le t\le m^{-\sigma}G^2.$$ Assume also that $\rho_{\mathrm{fc},t}(0)=1/\pi$. For every fixed positive integer $a$ and $O\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^a)$, the difference $$\begin{aligned}
 \label{eq:dbm-marginal-test}
 &\int_{\mathbb R^a}O(\boldsymbol\alpha)
   p_{V+\sqrt t\,W_m}^{(a)}
      \left(\frac{\pi\alpha_1}{m},\ldots,
            \frac{\pi\alpha_a}{m}\right)
       \,d\boldsymbol\alpha \notag\\
 &\hspace{12mm}-
 \int_{\mathbb R^a}O(\boldsymbol\alpha)
   p_{W_m}^{(a)}
      \left(\frac{\pi\alpha_1}{m},\ldots,
            \frac{\pi\alpha_a}{m}\right)
       \,d\boldsymbol\alpha
 \longrightarrow0.
\end{aligned}$$ Consequently, the expected sums of $O$ over ordered tuples of distinct points of the two eigenvalue processes, both rescaled by $m/\pi$, differ by $o(1)$.
